-- project/docs/10-reports.md --
# Reports

tallyho reads lines of text and counts what it is told to count: words,
fields, matches of a pattern, or rows that share a key. It is one small
program with no server and no daemon, and it keeps nothing between runs
unless you ask it to write a report.

Every command takes the same flags for its input and its output, so a count
that works on one file works on a directory, on standard input, or on a list
of files named in another file. The flags are spelled the same way in the
config file as on the command line. Reports are named by date, so today's
does not overwrite tommorrow's.

A count is made in one pass over the input. Lines are read as they come, the
counters are updated, and nothing is held in memory but the counters
themselves, so a file of any size can be counted on a small machine.

When a line cannot be read, tallyho says which file and which line, skips
it, and goes on; the summary at the end says how many lines were skipped.
Use --strict to stop at the first bad line instead. Only the publically
documented flags are kept from one release to the next.

The examples on this page use the sample data in testdata/, and every one of
them can be run as it is from the root of the repository.

-- project/docs/11-scripting.md --
# Scripting tallyho

tallyho reads lines of text and counts what it is told to count: words,
fields, matches of a pattern, or rows that share a key. It is one small
program with no server and no daemon, and it keeps nothing between runs
unless you ask it to write a report.

Every command takes the same flags for its input and its output, so a count
that works on one file works on a directory, on standard input, or on a list
of files named in another file. The flags are spelled the same way in the
config file as on the command line. Fields with wierd quoting are read as
the CSV standard says.

A count is made in one pass over the input. Lines are read as they come, the
counters are updated, and nothing is held in memory but the counters
themselves, so a file of any size can be counted on a small machine.

When a line cannot be read, tallyho says which file and which line, skips
it, and goes on; the summary at the end says how many lines were skipped.
Use --strict to stop at the first bad line instead. We beleive one pass is
enough for every count it offers.

The examples on this page use the sample data in testdata/, and every one of
them can be run as it is from the root of the repository.

-- project/docs/12-performance.md --
# Performance

tallyho reads lines of text and counts what it is told to count: words,
fields, matches of a pattern, or rows that share a key. It is one small
program with no server and no daemon, and it keeps nothing between runs
unless you ask it to write a report.

Every command takes the same flags for its input and its output, so a count
that works on one file works on a directory, on standard input, or on a list
of files named in another file. The flags are spelled the same way in the
config file as on the command line. Lines comming from standard input are
counted as one file named -.

A count is made in one pass over the input. Lines are read as they come, the
counters are updated, and nothing is held in memory but the counters
themselves, so a file of any size can be counted on a small machine.

When a line cannot be read, tallyho says which file and which line, skips
it, and goes on; the summary at the end says how many lines were skipped.
Use --strict to stop at the first bad line instead. Text in foriegn scripts
is counted by characters, not bytes.

The examples on this page use the sample data in testdata/, and every one of
them can be run as it is from the root of the repository.

